# Review package: batch 5, slice sni

## a35f81f Route each connection by its TLS server name and pass company traffic through unread

hornhoof.org could read every request and answer of every company, because the browser's https ended in Caddy. Now frps
takes port 443 and routes by the TLS server name. A company's https proxy gets the encrypted stream as it came, and the
visitor sees the company's own certificate; the relay holds no key for it. Everything else, hornhoof.org, www, reserved,
blocked, unknown and http-mode names and connections with no server name, goes through the droplet's own frpc (client
edge) to Caddy, now on 127.0.0.1:8443, with a PROXY v2 header that keeps the visitor's address, which Caddy believes only
from 127.0.0.1. Tunnels keep coming in as wss through that same way, so today's frpc settings stay valid.

The plugin lets the edge in only with its token and client id, and it may offer only its one catch-all proxy, with no
routes row. Any other session may offer the http or the https proxy named after its binding, under the same per-name lock
and one route across both types; custom domains stay refused. Health adds passthrough, from the watcher's connect to
frps's https port. *.hornhoof.org answers X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow.

Proved by test_sni.py against real frps 0.71.1, frpc and Caddy 2.11.8 on loopback, and by test_edge_today.py, which now runs
through frps and edge. The red runs failed on `only http proxies are allowed`, a missing passthrough and a missing header.

Ruling: frps's log.level on the droplet is error, where the plan keeps info, and README says frps logs only its errors in place of "frps logs each tunnel's login" - why: frps names each visitor's real address at info (server/proxy/proxy.go:260, seen in its log) and a refused one's at warn (proxy.go:283, which Task 10's NewUserConn reaches), so the README's "which it does not keep" would be false in the journal; test_the_droplet_logs_no_visitor_address failed on this and passes now; old tests read info lines, so the harness keeps info unless droplet_logging is set - cost if wrong: an operator loses frps's login and warning lines in the journal; one line brings them back, and then the README's promise must change.
Ruling: one line added to Task 8's test_refused_connections_read_as_failures (stop_frps), every assertion unchanged, where the plan says Task 8's tests stay unchanged - why: frps now holds the public port, so with only Caddy stopped the port stays open and the handshake fails with SSLEOFError, never ConnectionRefusedError; test_passthrough_needs_no_caddy pins what happens with only Caddy gone - cost if wrong: none, the test still proves what it did.
Ruling: test_caddy_https_on_loopback_only reads test Caddy's https port from /proc/net/tcp and tcp6, not ss -ltnH, and "http on every address" from caddy adapt of the repo's own Caddyfile (test_droplet_caddy_listens_as_the_plan_says) - why: test Caddy's http port stays on loopback by Task 8's ruling and its test, as this family's machine shows no test server to its network; home-api's image installs no iproute2 - cost if wrong: Caddy's reading of the file is proved, the real bind only at the deploy.
Ruling: test_r7_3's xff is 127.0.0.1, 127.0.0.1, not the plan's 127.0.0.1 - why: Task 8's ruling, Caddy names the visitor and frps adds the address it got the request from - cost if wrong: none.
Ruling: the Caddyfile has an explicit http:// site on port 80, and each https site binds 127.0.0.1 - why: Caddy's automatic redirect listens where its site binds, so it would be on loopback only; on Linux it joins the site on :80 (Caddy 2.11.8 modules/caddyhttp/server.go:1010) and HTTP-01 is answered ahead of any handler (server.go:709), read in the source and not run, since that needs a real ACME server - cost if wrong: the certificates of hornhoof.org and www do not renew by HTTP-01, seen at the deploy; test_stub shows the redirect itself works.
Ruling: the message 'only http proxies are allowed' stays for other types although https is allowed too - why: test_today.py and test_peers.py pin it, and old tests are not rewritten - cost if wrong: a slightly narrow message to a client that is refused anyway.
Ruling: the edge's NewProxy also refuses a subdomain, group, locations and route_by_http_user, beyond the plan's three fields, and a session with metas.edge never falls back to a binding's checks, Ping and other ops included - why: R1.2, and fail closed - cost if wrong: none, the real edge sends none of them.
Ruling: the harness gives edge frpc its token in the environment, not rendered into the file as the plan says, and a Stack with no Caddy sets neither RELAY_FRPS_HTTPS nor RELAY_EDGE_TOKEN - why: the file's own template is then what runs, as on the droplet, and that is the plan's "a stack started without" - cost if wrong: none.
Ruling: proxyBindAddr is 0.0.0.0 as the plan says, so port 443 is IPv4 only, where Caddy's was on both - why: the plan's value; the ledger says the ops note lists no IPv6 address - cost if wrong: if hornhoof.org has an AAAA record, its IPv6 visitors fail; for the deploy to check.
Ruling: left as it is: Caddy's error log names a visitor's address when a request fails on its way to frps or the relay, and README now says so - why: old behaviour, off the path of an https company, which is what the README's promise covers; silencing it would hide real faults and the plan does not ask it - cost if wrong: an http-mode visitor's address can sit in the journal after a failure until that company updates.

